2026-07-30  Doug Blank <doug.blank@gmail.com>

	* gramps/gen/fs/utils/__init__.py, gramps/gen/fs/utils/linking.py,
	gramps/gui/fs/actions.py, gramps/gui/fs/compare/window.py: Persist
	FamilySearch ID links independent of editor Save/Cancel Linking a FamilySearch ID (via the Link dialog or as a side effect
	of Compare) only ever updated the live in-memory Person from an open
	editor; nothing was committed unless the user later clicked OK. If
	they clicked Cancel instead, the link was silently lost even though
	the user had explicitly made it, leaving inconsistent FamilySearch
	links.  Add link_gramps_fs_id_by_handle(), which attaches/updates the
	_FSFTID attribute on the database's own copy of the person and
	commits it right away, independent of whatever else is unsaved in a
	live editor for the same person. Both the Link dialog and
	CompareWindow now use it, so a link, once made, is always saved,
	while every other in-progress edit still waits for the editor's own
	OK/Cancel as before.

2026-07-30  Doug Blank <doug.blank@gmail.com>

	* gramps/gen/fs/session.py, gramps/gui/fs/compare/window.py: Avoid
	committing live editor edits; lock FS token refresh CompareWindow linked the FamilySearch ID onto whatever Person object
	it was given, which could be the live, in-progress object from an
	open Edit Person dialog. Committing that object as a side effect of
	opening the compare window could silently persist unsaved editor
	changes before the user clicked OK. CompareWindow now loads a fresh
	copy of the person from the database and commits that instead.  Session.refresh_access_token() also had no locking, so concurrent
	worker threads (e.g. the notes/sources ThreadPoolExecutor added
	earlier in this branch) that each hit a 401 around the same time
	could all fire their own refresh request for the same login. A lock
	now serializes refreshes, with a double-check so a thread that loses
	the race reuses the token a sibling thread already fetched instead
	of requesting a second one.

2026-07-18  Doug Blank <doug.blank@gmail.com>

	* gramps/gui/fs/fs_import/__init__.py,
	gramps/gui/fs/fs_import/importer.py: Use ngettext for
	count-dependent strings in FamilySearch import log Nick-Hall flagged several progress/debug messages introduced in this
	branch that interpolate a person count into "... people" without
	using ngettext, so translations can't produce a grammatically
	correct singular form when the count is 1 (e.g. "1 people"). Fixed
	the three flagged occurrences (ancestor/descendant generation
	downloads, and the comparison-status refresh count) plus two more of
	the same pattern found while auditing the rest of the file (spouse
	download, and the Last-Modified timestamp resolution count/summary).  Export ngettext from gramps.gui.fs.fs_import, alongside the existing
	_ alias, since it's now used across multiple modules in the package.  Co-Authored-By: Doug Blank <doug.blank@gmail.com> Co-Authored-By:
	Claude Sonnet 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>

2026-07-18  Doug Blank <doug.blank@gmail.com>

	* gramps/gen/fs/compare/__init__.py,
	gramps/gen/fs/compare/aggregate.py,
	gramps/gen/fs/compare/test/aggregate_test.py,
	gramps/gui/fs/fs_import/importer.py: Parallelize Last-Modified
	backfill during comparison-status refresh compare_fs_to_gramps() issues a synchronous FamilySearch HEAD
	request per person, one at a time, whenever a person's Last-Modified
	wasn't already captured during download -- confirmed via the debug
	output added previously: for a real 1849-person import, many
	individual people were taking 1-2.5s each in this comparison-refresh
	pass, serially, with no way to overlap them.  Add backfill_last_modified() to gramps/gen/fs/compare/aggregate.py,
	which resolves Last-Modified/Etag (following FSID redirects) for a
	batch of (fs_person, gr_person) pairs concurrently via a thread
	pool, matching the pattern already used for notes/sources downloads
	and Tree.add_persons. Network I/O runs in worker threads; the
	resulting FSID relinks and database writes still happen on the
	calling thread.  The GUI importer now runs this backfill once, concurrently, right
	before the comparison loop, reusing the same (fs_person, gr_person)
	pairs for both passes instead of re-deriving them per person.
	compare_fs_to_gramps() is unchanged: it still resolves Last-Modified
	itself when called standalone (e.g. the single-person Compare
	button), but during bulk import it now finds the value already
	cached and skips its own per-person network call entirely.  Co-Authored-By: Doug Blank <doug.blank@gmail.com> Co-Authored-By:
	Claude Sonnet 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>

2026-07-18  Doug Blank <doug.blank@gmail.com>

	* gramps/gui/fs/fs_import/importer.py: Report per-person progress
	during ancestor/descendant/spouse download Each ancestor/descendant/spouse download phase advanced the progress
	bar by a single tick per generation, regardless of how many people
	that generation actually involved. A deep pull's later generations
	can contain hundreds or thousands of people, so the bar (and cursor)
	sat visibly frozen for the entire time it took to fetch a
	generation, even though the console kept printing one line per
	generation and the download was proceeding normally underneath.  Switch these three passes to activity mode and pass progress.step
	itself as Tree.add_parents/add_children/add_spouses'
	progress_callback, so the bar pulses (and GTK events get pumped)
	once per person actually fetched rather than once per generation.
	Also print each generation's frontier size before fetching it, and
	update the progress dialog's header text per generation, so both the
	console and the GUI show concrete, moving feedback throughout a
	large pull.  Co-Authored-By: Doug Blank <doug.blank@gmail.com> Co-Authored-By:
	Claude Sonnet 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>

2026-07-18  Doug Blank <doug.blank@gmail.com>

	* gramps/gui/fs/fs_import/importer.py: Keep GUI responsive during
	FamilySearch comparison-status refresh The post-import "refresh comparison status" pass closed the progress
	dialog and cursor before running, then looped over every downloaded
	person with no progress reporting and no GTK event pumping -- the
	same class of freeze already fixed for the download phases. This
	pass can be slow: compare_fs_to_gramps() may issue a synchronous
	FamilySearch HEAD request per person when Last-Modified wasn't
	already captured during download, and the whole loop was wrapped in
	a single bare except that silently aborted every remaining
	comparison on the first error, with no log output at all.  Keep the progress dialog open through this pass so progress.step()
	pumps GTK events as before, move the per-person exception handling
	inside the loop so one failure doesn't abort the rest, and log
	failures instead of swallowing them. Also add debug print
	statements: periodic done/total progress, a warning when a single
	person's comparison takes over a second (to surface slow/stalled
	FamilySearch requests), and a final summary of elapsed time plus
	compared/failed counts.  Co-Authored-By: Doug Blank <doug.blank@gmail.com> Co-Authored-By:
	Claude Sonnet 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>

2026-07-18  Doug Blank <doug.blank@gmail.com>

	* gramps/gen/fs/test/tree_test.py, gramps/gen/fs/tree.py,
	gramps/gui/fs/fs_import/importer.py: Keep GUI responsive during
	FamilySearch bulk import downloads Every download phase of a bulk import (persons, ancestors,
	descendants, spouses, and notes/sources) fetches concurrently via a
	ThreadPoolExecutor, but the main GTK thread just blocked on
	as_completed() until the whole batch finished, without returning
	control to the event loop. For a large import this could block long
	enough that the window manager decided the app had hung and offered
	to force-quit it, even though the download was proceeding normally.  Add an optional progress_callback to Tree.add_persons() (and
	add_parents/add_children/add_spouses, which delegate to it), invoked
	once per completed fetch. The GUI layer passes in a callback that
	pumps pending GTK events, and the notes/sources fetch loop does the
	same directly, so the interface stays responsive throughout the
	download.  Co-Authored-By: Doug Blank <doug.blank@gmail.com> Co-Authored-By:
	Claude Sonnet 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>

2026-07-18  Doug Blank <doug.blank@gmail.com>

	* gramps/gui/fs/fs_import/importer.py: Parallelize notes/sources
	download during bulk import The notes/sources phase of a bulk import fetched notes, sources, and
	memories for every downloaded person and couple relationship one
	HTTP request at a time -- 3 requests per person plus 2 per couple,
	fully serial. For a large import this was by far the slowest phase,
	since every other phase (persons, ancestors, descendants, spouses)
	already fetches each generation's batch concurrently via a thread
	pool in Tree.add_persons.  Apply the same pattern here: fetch all the raw JSON payloads
	concurrently with up to 8 worker threads, then deserialize them
	sequentially in the main thread, since deserializing mutates shared
	tree state that isn't safe to touch from multiple threads at once.  Co-Authored-By: Doug Blank <doug.blank@gmail.com> Co-Authored-By:
	Claude Sonnet 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>

2026-07-18  Doug Blank <doug.blank@gmail.com>

	* gramps/gen/fs/compare/aggregate.py,
	gramps/gui/fs/fs_import/importer.py: Fix bulk import hanging
	indefinitely during notes/sources download The post-import comparison-status refresh in FSToGrampsImporter
	called compare_fs_to_gramps() once per imported person, and that
	function opened its own database transaction on every call. For a
	large bulk import this meant thousands of separate commits, each one
	firing a live GUI update signal and adding a new entry to the undo
	history, instead of a single batched update. This produced a very
	long freeze after "import done." was printed, and could exhaust
	memory on large imports as the undo history grew without bound.  Add an optional txn parameter to compare_fs_to_gramps() (and thread
	it through to FSStatusDB.commit(), which already accepted one) so
	callers can share a single transaction across many people. The bulk
	importer now wraps the whole refresh loop in one transaction with
	signals suppressed, and only rebuilds the GUI once at the end.  Co-Authored-By: Doug Blank <doug.blank@gmail.com> Co-Authored-By:
	Claude Sonnet 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>

2026-07-18  Doug Blank <doug.blank@gmail.com>

	* gramps/gen/fs/session.py, gramps/gui/fs/fs_import/importer.py: Fix
	bulk import hanging indefinitely during notes/sources download The FamilySearch Session class never set a request timeout, so a
	single stalled connection during the notes/sources download phase of
	a bulk import could hang forever with no way to recover short of
	killing the app. Add a default 30 second timeout (overridable via
	GRAMPS_FS_API_TIMEOUT) applied centrally in _route_api_request, the
	common path for all API calls. Timeouts are already handled
	gracefully by get_jsonurl, which logs and returns None.  Also print progress periodically while downloading notes and
	sources, since that phase can involve thousands of sequential
	requests for a deep bulk import and previously printed nothing until
	it finished, making it look stuck even when working normally.  Co-Authored-By: Doug Blank <doug.blank@gmail.com> Co-Authored-By:
	Claude Sonnet 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>

