2026-05-02  Ian Davis <18375+iand@users.noreply.github.com>

	* gramps/gen/lib/baseobj.py, gramps/gen/lib/family.py,
	gramps/gen/lib/place.py, gramps/gen/lib/test/handleref_test.py,
	gramps/plugins/test/tools_test.py, po/POTFILES.skip: Add tests for
	referential integrity of deletes and fix 3 bugs This is based on issue #9895 which reported that not all references
	are removed when some objects are deleted. This issue is nine years
	old so probably some of these situations have been fixed in the
	meantime.  dsblank suggested writing some tests to uncover any remaining bugs
	and that's what this change does. It adds a range of tests for
	get_referenced_handles_recursively() and remove_handle_references()
	and in the process found three bugs which I have tried to fix: Bug 1: Family.get_referenced_handles_recursively() double-counts
	child person handles.  Family.get_referenced_handles() explicitly included ("Person",
	ref.ref) for each entry in child_ref_list. But child_ref_list is
	also returned by Family.get_handle_referents(), and each
	ChildRef.get_referenced_handles() independently returns ("Person",
	self.ref). The recursive traversal therefore sees each child person
	handle twice. Fixed by removing [ref.ref for ref in
	self.child_ref_list] from Family.get_referenced_handles().  Bug 2: Place.remove_handle_references("Place", ...) silently did
	nothing.  _remove_handle_references was never implemented on Place so calles
	fall through to the base class no-op, leaving stale PlaceRef entries
	behind when a place is deleted. Fixed by implementing
	remove_handle_references Bug 3: get_referenced_handles_recursively() returns duplicate
	handles when the same handle is referenced at multiple levels The accumulation in BaseObject.get_referenced_handles_recursively()
	is a plain list append with no deduplication. If the same handle
	appeared at both the top-level object (e.g. person.note_list) and in
	a child secondary object (e.g. EventRef.note_list), or in two
	sibling secondary objects, it was emitted once per occurrence. The
	database backend wraps the result in set() so it was unaffected, but
	the function's contract implies uniqueness.  Fixed by replacing the list accumulation in
	BaseObject.get_referenced_handles_recursively() with a seen set that
	filters duplicates as they are collected, preserving insertion
	order.

